    "answer": {
      "short": "Put $u=\\sqrt[nd]{a}$. Then $(u^d)^n=a$, so $u^d=\\sqrt[n]{a}$.\n\nThus $(\\sqrt[nd]{a})^{md}=(u^d)^m=(\\sqrt[n]{a})^m$.",
      "worked": "Put $u=\\sqrt[nd]{a}\\geq0$. Using integer-power laws,\n$$\\begin{align*}(u^d)^n&=u^{dn}\\\\(u^d)^n&=a.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $u^d\\geq0$, uniqueness of the non-negative $n$th root gives $u^d=\\sqrt[n]{a}$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\sqrt[nd]{a})^{md}&=u^{md}\\\\(\\sqrt[nd]{a})^{md}&=(u^d)^m\\\\(\\sqrt[nd]{a})^{md}&=(\\sqrt[n]{a})^m.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
